# Git Commands

## clone
Copies a remote repository to your computer.

Example:
```bash
git clone <repository-url>
```

---

## checkout
Switches branches or restores files.

Example:
```bash
git checkout main
```

---

## -b
Creates a new branch when used with checkout.

Example:
```bash
git checkout -b new-branch
```

---

## status
Shows the current state of files in the repository.

Example:
```bash
git status
```

---

## push
Uploads local commits to GitHub.

Example:
```bash
git push
```

---

## -u
Sets the upstream branch when pushing.

Example:
```bash
git push -u origin main
```

---

## pull
Downloads changes from GitHub and merges them.

Example:
```bash
git pull
```

---

## add
Stages files for commit.

Example:
```bash
git add file.txt
```

---

## commit
Saves staged changes to the repository history.

Example:
```bash
git commit
```

---

## -m
Adds a commit message.

Example:
```bash
git commit -m "Added new feature"
```

---

## -a
Stages modified tracked files automatically.

Example:
```bash
git commit -a
```

---

## -am
Stages tracked files and adds a commit message.

Example:
```bash
git commit -am "Updated files"
```

---

## branch
Lists or creates branches.

Example:
```bash
git branch
```

---

## branch -a
Shows all local and remote branches.

Example:
```bash
git branch -a
```

---

## branch -d
Deletes a branch.

Example:
```bash
git branch -d old-branch
```

---

## merge
Combines changes from one branch into another.

Example:
```bash
git merge feature-branch
```
